    /// <summary>Make a conceptual drawing set of the model as the list's earlier changes leave it, each view placed on a numbered sheet of its own, which the job prints with the model's other printable sheets into its sheets PDF (sheets.pdf), in sheet-number order. A set that says nothing makes a floor plan of every level (a level nothing stands on included), the four elevations, two sections through the middle of the building (east-west, then north-south) and a 3D view: sheets A-101 on for the plans, lowest level first, A-201 to A-204 for the elevations (north, east, south, west), A-301 and A-302 for the sections, A-401 for the 3D view. A sheet number or view name the model already holds is never reused: the next free number of the same series is taken, and a view name gets (2), (3) .... Each sheet takes the title block named by title_block, or the first title block the model holds (by family, then type), or no title block when the model holds none (a sheet of no title block is a 36 x 24 in sheet, or 841 x 594 mm in a metric model, for the layout). Each view is cropped to the building with a margin and given a scale chosen to fit the sheet: the largest standard scale (1/4" = 1'-0" to 1/64" = 1'-0", or 1 : 50 to 1 : 5000 in a metric model) at which it fits the sheet's usable area (the title block's box less a margin and the title strip at its right), and its viewport is placed at that area's middle. The building is the extent of its walls, floors, roofs, ceilings, columns, framing, foundations, stairs, railings, doors, windows and generic models: a model with none gets its plans and no elevation, section or 3D view, saying why. The views and sheets are drawings of the model, not content of it: the set adds no building element, so a new model's first list needs more than a set. Its options ask for less, never for another kind of view: views, the kinds to make; levels, the levels whose plans are made; empty_levels; title_block. The report says, for each view and sheet, what was made (view, sheet, viewport and elevation marker, each by element id), its scale, where its viewport sits on the sheet and whether it fits, and why anything was not made, placed or fitted. A view the add-in cannot make is reported and the others go on; a set none of whose views could be made is rolled back. Since change-list version 8 a set's plans also tag their rooms: on each plan, every room Revit placed on its level, at its point, with the model's default room tag type (or the first it holds when it has no default; a model that holds none still gets its plans, each saying why its rooms are not tagged, and a tag Revit refuses is counted out while the others go on), and a version 8 set that says nothing also makes a room schedule on A-601 (room_schedule), which comes after the 3D view, and a cover on A-001 (cover: a sheet index of every sheet the model holds and a note saying what the set is), which sheets.pdf prints first. The report then says, for each plan, how many rooms are placed on its level and how many it tagged, and why when fewer; for the room schedule its sheet, its segments and how many rooms it lists; and for the cover its sheet, its index's segments, how many sheets it lists and whether its note was placed. A set of a version 7 list is as it always was: no room tag, no room schedule and no cover. Put it last, after what it draws. Needs change-list version 7; its room tags, room schedule and cover, version 8.</summary>
